  /* ══ D231: THE STOPWATCH STARTS THE NEXT STEP (owner, 2026-10-07) ══════
     "If I say LT pull for 200 ms it does that, then after 200 ms it goes to
     the next one, so if it's B 80 ms it does B after the 200 ms of LT -
     UNLESS someone puts a stopwatch: LT 200 ms, then the stopwatch 20 ms,
     then B 80 ms - then LT is held, after 20 ms LT will still be held for
     another 180 but B will then start for 80."

     So in the editor's model (`a.steps`, timing 2):
       · a step with a length runs it, and the next step starts when it ends;
       · a button press (tap, or a trigger pull that lets go) FOLLOWED BY A
         WAIT keeps its own full length, and the wait says when the NEXT step
         starts, counted from the start of that press.
     The engine runs one step at a time (zs_actions.c), so compileSteps turns
     that timeline into steps it already has: hold at the start, let go at
     the end, waits for the gaps. Nothing on the Core changes and nothing is
     flashed. An action whose steps have no press-then-wait is sent exactly
     as authored, byte for byte as before.

     The old meaning of a wait after a press was "a gap after it ended".
     Every action that does not carry timing 2 (stored, on a board, a share
     code, a Ready-made's build, a recording) is converted ONCE in normAction
     by adding the press's length to the wait that follows it, so it runs
     exactly as it always did. A converted action that has an overlap travels
     with its authored steps beside the compiled ones (toBoard: `authored`,
     `timing`), which the board's validator carries untouched like `notes`. */
  function timedPress(s) {
    return !!s && (s.k === 'tap' || (s.k === 'trig' && !s.keep)) && intOf(s.ms, 0) > 0;
  }
  /* a REAL overlap: the stopwatch after a press is shorter than the press,
     so the next step starts while it is still held. A stopwatch as long as
     the press or longer overlaps nothing - it is the old "press, then a
     gap" - and is sent in exactly the old form (oldForm), byte for byte,
     so no existing config grows (the D124 copy must still fit 16 KB). */
  function overlapsIn(steps) {
    for (var i = 0; i + 1 < steps.length; i++) {
      if (timedPress(steps[i]) && steps[i + 1] && steps[i + 1].k === 'wait' &&
          intOf(steps[i + 1].ms, 0) < intOf(steps[i].ms, 0)) return true;
    }
    return false;
